= Persistent state contamination (artificial intelligence) — Technical =

== State lifecycle ==
Let a memory/state item <code>m</code> pass through:

<pre>
source
→ candidate write
→ validation
→ storage/versioning
→ retrieval
→ interpretation
→ action
→ derived write
</pre>

Contamination occurs when an invalid or mis-scoped item survives into durable state and later has causal influence.

== Integrity dimensions ==
A durable item can be contaminated because of:

* false content;
* malicious instruction;
* stale validity;
* wrong identity binding;
* wrong scope;
* broken provenance;
* corrupted derivation;
* unauthorized authority;
* unresolved contradiction.

A binary true/false field is therefore insufficient for many agent-memory systems.

== Provenance-carrying state ==
A richer item can be represented as:

<pre>
m = (content, source, subject, scope, time, version, derivation, authority, confidence)
</pre>

These fields do not guarantee correctness. They make later trust decisions and rollback more tractable.

== Memory poisoning ==
Adversarial memory poisoning supplies content that the agent writes now and retrieves later.

The security boundary is unusual because the agent itself may transform the hostile source through summarization or tool use before storing it. This can obscure origin.

Recent work therefore studies write-time origin binding, tamper-evident provenance, retrieval screening, and lifecycle governance.

== Contamination propagation ==
A contaminated item can create descendants:

<pre>
m_bad
→ action a
→ result r
→ summary s
→ m_bad2
</pre>

Deleting the original item may not remove derived contamination unless lineage is tracked.

== Recovery ==
Recovery can require:

* identify contaminated roots;
* compute/inspect descendants;
* quarantine dependent state;
* restore from a trusted version;
* replay from clean evidence;
* invalidate derived caches;
* re-evaluate actions taken under contaminated state.

This resembles data-lineage recovery more than simple prompt filtering.

== Shared memory ==
In multi-agent systems, shared memory creates a propagation surface.

One agent can write an invalid item that is later consumed by several agents, multiplying the blast radius. Subject/scope binding and write authority are therefore separate from semantic relevance.

== Security evidence boundary ==
Published 2026 memory-poisoning results demonstrate real cross-session persistence in tested systems, but attack rates vary by harness, model, memory implementation, and threat model.

The broader "persistent state contamination" category also includes accidental errors and should not inherit adversarial benchmark numbers without evidence.

